# Team task estimate (`y-team-task-estimate`)

Use when the user wants a **story-point estimate** for an **existing** tracker item, or invokes **`/y-team-task-estimate`** / **`@y-team-task-estimate`**.

**Audiences:** **Dev team**, **QA team**, or **both** (default **both**). Dev and QA are **two capacity buckets**, not one combined team SP. QA points are **QA work only** (cases, run, retest, report), not implementation.

**Not** ticket creation — that is [`y-team-task-refinement.mdc`](y-team-task-refinement.mdc). **Not** an implementation plan — that is `y-agt-plan-phase`.

Keep it **fast** (startup): enough context to pick a number, not a workshop.

---

## AskQuestion

Use **`AskQuestion`** for every gate below. If the tool is **unavailable**: say so in **one line**, then ask the **same options in chat** and **wait**. Do not skip gates.

---

## 0b. Short intro (mandatory)

**Before** fetching Jira, post a short intro. Do **not** AskQuestion on the intro unless the user objects.

Cover, in tight bullets (close to this length — do not lecture):

```
Quick estimate for this ticket.

- Scale: Fibonacci 1 2 3 5 8. Relative size, **not** hours or days.
- Why not time: (1) we compare to similar work, not a clock; (2) hours ignore risk/unknowns and fake a deadline.
- I weigh complexity, effort (incl. review/QA), risk, and unknowns.
- AI speeds implementation; unknowns and QA still count.
- I always give a Fibonacci number **and** a confidence score **0–5** (low = less accurate). Split and spike are recommendations beside the number, not the estimate itself. Honest size above 8 → recommend split (cap the number at 8).
- I glance at related code (can be more than one repo). If I cannot open a repo, I ask you for it.
```

Do **not** paste a factor table or a long methodology.

---

## 0b2. Audience (mandatory AskQuestion)

Immediately **after** the intro, **`AskQuestion`** (multi-select; **pre-select both** when the tool allows):

| Option | Path |
|--------|------|
| **Dev team** | Current Dev factors, **Story Point estimate**, labels `y-ai-estimated` / `y-ai-est-confidence-<n>` |
| **QA team** | QA factor meanings, **QA Story Point**, labels `y-ai-qa-storypoint` / `y-ai-qa-sp-confidence-<n>` |

If **AskQuestion** is unavailable: same options in chat and **wait**.

When **QA** or **Both** is selected, add this in chat (short, no table):

```
QA uses the same four factor names (complexity, effort, risk, unknowns) with QA evaluation (oracles, evidence, env). Same Fibonacci 1 2 3 5 8 and confidence 0–5. Vague tickets still get a number plus low confidence. Hours on a calendar do not change the Jira points.
```

Keep the Dev intro wording above when **Dev-only**. Do not replace Dev **Effort** (glue, tests, review, QA) on the Dev path.

---

## 0c. Ticket required (hard stop)

Do **not** gather or recommend a number without a ticket reference.

**Accept:**

- Jira key (`VAP-1234`)
- Jira browse URL (`https://vendoroo-dev.atlassian.net/browse/VAP-1234`)

**If missing:** **AskQuestion** — paste key / paste URL / cancel. **Stop** until they provide one.

**Do not:**

- Estimate from a free-text idea with no ticket
- Infer the ticket **only** from the git branch unless the user confirms that key in the same turn
- Invent a key

If the key/URL is invalid or **`getJiraIssue`** fails, say so and ask for a correct ref — do not guess.

---

## 1. Gather (read-only, time-boxed)

Use the **confirmed** key. Pull:

- Summary, description, AC, type, status, recent comments (not the entire history)
- Linked issues: **Blocked by** / **blocks** (cannot start; QA cannot test) **and** **Requires in Production** / **Is Required in Production by** (can QA on `testing`; must not cherry-pick to production alone). Do **not** treat those two link types as the same. Also clones when present.
- Attachments / linked docs **only if** they would change the number

Do **not** crawl Confluence or start a long investigation. If a gap would change `1` vs `3` vs `8`, go to step 2.

Jira: Atlassian MCP, `cloudId: vendoroo-dev.atlassian.net`, `getJiraIssue` with `responseContentFormat: "markdown"`. Linked issues via issue links / JQL as needed.

---

## 1b. Related code (one or more repos)

When **Dev** is selected (alone or with QA): code context makes the **Dev** estimate more accurate. Identify **which projects** the work likely touches (description, links, service names, `github.com/...` URLs, workspace folders).

A ticket **may map to more than one repo**. Give **one combined Dev number** unless the user asks to split tickets.

**Look (fast, not a full review):**

- Current Cursor workspace / matching sibling folders
- GitHub URLs on the ticket
- `gh` / remotes if already cloned

Look at **entry points, similar existing code, and obvious blast radius** — not every file.

**No access:**

- Name the repo(s) you **cannot** open and **why** (not in workspace, private, missing clone, `gh` denied).
- **AskQuestion:** local path / open the folder in Cursor / clone URL / skip that repo (lower **Dev confidence**).
- **Do not** invent module structure or pretend you read code you did not see.
- If a skipped repo is likely **large** complexity, lower **confidence** and note the gap — still give a best-effort Fibonacci number. You may **recommend** split or spike beside that number.

When **QA-only:** do **not** do a deep “implement this” review. Glance at product behavior only to judge **blast radius** (surfaces, oracles). Also look at AC, comments, **Requires in Production** links, and existing test docs under `docs/<KEY>/` when present.

---

## 2. Clarify only blockers

**AskQuestion** (or recommend a ~10-minute sync) only when **any** of these would move the number:

- Unclear done / missing AC
- Unknown owner of a dependency
- Open product decision
- Hidden scope (“also refactor X”)

Skip questions that would not change `1` vs `3` vs `8`.

---

## 3. Score four factors (mental — not a long table in chat)

Each factor **S / M / L**. Combine with judgment (guideline, not a formula):

| Factor | Meaning (AI-era) |
|--------|------------------|
| **Complexity** | New design vs known pattern — not lines of code |
| **Effort** | Remaining **human** work: glue, tests, review, QA — typing is usually cheap |
| **Risk** | Production / data / rollback |
| **Unknowns** | Need a spike or a meeting before a fair number |

- Mostly **S** → `1` or `2`
- Mixed **M** → `3` or `5`
- Any **L** as one ticket → `8` and **recommend split** if honest size is above 8
- Unknowns **L** → still give a Fibonacci number with **low confidence (0–2)**; **recommend** spike or split beside it — do not pretend unknowns are small

**Cap:** `1, 2, 3, 5, 8` only. Do **not** use 13 or 21. If honest size is above 8 → **Estimate is 8** and **recommend split**. Do not replace the Estimate field with the word “split”.

**Spike:** if the work is mostly investigation, **Estimate** is still `1`–`3` (size of the ticket as written). **Recommend** treating it as a spike and say what the spike must answer. Do not set Estimate to the word “spike”.

**Forbidden:** do **not** inflate Story Points from confidence (no `final SP = initial × (1 / confidence)`, no non-Fibonacci results such as 4). Confidence does not change the Estimate number.

**AI-era:** do not inflate because “coding used to take days.” Unknowns, review, QA, and product back-and-forth still count.

**Relative size:** `3` means “about like other 3s,” not three hours.

### QA factor meanings (when QA or Both)

Same **S / M / L**, same combine guide, same Fibonacci `1 2 3 5 8`. **Different questions** (do not paste this table in chat):

| Factor | QA meaning |
|--------|------------|
| **Complexity** | How hard is it to **know pass/fail**? Oracles, multi-turn flows, data combinations, many surfaces. Not lines of product code. |
| **Effort** | Remaining **human QA** work: criteria/cases, data/env setup, execution, evidence, retest, report. Not coding the feature. |
| **Risk** | Cost of a **missed fail** (prod, data, payments, PII). Include QA on the `testing` mix vs production cherry-pick when other unreleased work may be involved. Flag **Requires in Production** / **Is Required in Production by**. Do **not** use **Blocked by** for that. |
| **Unknowns** | Thin AC, no oracle, no env, unclear test data, missing **Requires in Production** when the stack needs it. Do not treat **Blocked by** as the same signal. |

**Vague AC / no oracle:** still pick `1 2 3 5 8` with **QA confidence 0–2** and recommend refinement or a short exploratory pass. Do **not** skip the QA estimate until the ticket is clean.

People may keep **hours** on a calendar for QA execution. That does **not** change **QA Story Point**.

Dev and QA remain **separate** numbers. Do not add them into one SP.

---

## 3b. Confidence score 0–5 (mandatory)

Always output **Confidence: N/5** alongside **each** estimate you produce (Dev, QA, or both). **Never leave an estimate blank** because the ticket is vague — give a **best-effort Fibonacci number** and use **confidence** to show how reliable it is.

When **Both**, output **two** Fibonacci numbers and **two** confidence scores (gaps can differ).

Score from what you **have** vs what is **missing**:

| Score | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| **5** | Ticket is refined and clear: solid AC, enough context, repos checked, few gaps |
| **4** | Mostly clear; minor gaps that are unlikely to move the number |
| **3** | Usable estimate; some vagueness, missing detail, or unchecked repo |
| **2** | Significant gaps (thin AC, open product call, skipped important repo) — number is directional |
| **1** | Very vague ticket — best-effort only; recommend refinement or a short sync |
| **0** | Almost no usable context — placeholder guess; strongly recommend **`/y-team-task-refinement`** before planning |

**How to treat the number for sprint load** (same bands for Dev and QA; official scale is **0–5**, not High/Medium/Low):

- **4–5 / 5** — treat the Estimate as sprint load
- **3 / 5** — usable; it might move
- **0–2 / 5** — still output the number; **do not treat it as a sure sprint load**; warn in **chat and the local estimate markdown** (not on the Jira issue)

**Raise the score when provided:** clear AC, recent decisive comments, linked tickets fetched, code paths seen, similar done tickets cited, assignee/owner known.

**Lower the score when missing:** no/thin AC, contradictory description, unknown blast radius, repo not opened, blocker not checked, open product fork, hidden scope, cannot pick between two Fibonacci steps.

In chat and the outcome file, add **Confidence drivers** (short bullets):

- **What we had** — what supported the estimate
- **Gaps** — what was missing or vague (each gap should map to a lower score)

If confidence is **0–2**, **tell the user** the estimate is low accuracy, list gaps, and offer **`AskQuestion`**: answer follow-ups now / run **`/y-team-task-refinement`** / accept as-is / keep a split-or-spike **recommendation**. **Always keep the Fibonacci Estimate.**

Cap follow-up at **two or three** rounds (startup pace). Re-score confidence after new answers.

Optional (local file and chat only, **never** Jira): if confidence is 0–2, you may note a **planning talk** round-up of **one** Fibonacci step (2 → 3, or 3 → 5) with a one-line reason. That is judgment, not a formula, and it is **not** the Estimate written to Jira.

---

## 4. Recommend (short chat)

Required shape **per selected audience**. When **Both**, use two labeled blocks (**Dev** then **QA**), each with its own Estimate, Confidence, Factors line, drivers, and Why.

- **Estimate:** `<1|2|3|5|8>` — **always** a Fibonacci number. Size of **the ticket as written** for that audience. Never the words `split` or `spike`.
- **Confidence:** `N/5` (integer **0–5** per step 3b) for **that** audience
- **Recommendations:** omit this block if none. Otherwise short bullets:
  - Split into … (named slices + suggested sizes)
  - Spike first: … (what it must answer)
  - Run `/y-team-task-refinement` (when confidence is 0–2 or AC is empty)
- **Confidence drivers:**
  - **What we had** — bullets (provided context that supported the number)
  - **Gaps** — bullets (vagueness, missing AC, repo access, unknowns — why the score is not higher)
- **Checked / considered:** a **short bullet list** of what you actually looked at. Always include:
  - **Repos** you opened (name them). If you skipped a repo, say so and why.
  - **Ticket context:** AC, type, recent comments, linked issues (only what you used).
  - **Blockers:** active and potential (Jira **Blocked by**; missing access; unclear done). **Requires in Production** is a **dependency**, not a start-blocker — list it under Dependencies.
  - **Dependencies:** active and potential (other tickets, services, people, releases).
  - Write `none seen` for a line if that bucket is empty — do not invent.
- **Why this number is reasonable:** a **short bullet list** (about 3–5 bullets). Explain the **outcome**, not a methodology lecture:
  - What is already known / similar in the codebase
  - What still costs human time (review, QA, glue, product)
  - Risk or unknowns that moved the number **up** or **down**
  - Why it is **not** the next Fibonacci step (e.g. not a `5` because …)

Keep bullets **short**. No multi-page analysis. Do **not** paste the S/M/L factor table.

Example:

```
**Estimate:** 3
**Confidence:** 2/5
**Recommendations:**
- Run /y-team-task-refinement — AC for the edge case is missing
**Confidence drivers:**
- What we had: summary + partial AC; one repo glanced
- Gaps: edge-case AC unclear; second repo skipped; no similar done ticket cited
**Checked / considered:**
- Repos: vendoroo-ai (follow-up language), resiroo — skipped (not in workspace)
- Ticket: AC + last 3 comments; linked VAP-xxxx (blocks)
- Blockers: VAP-xxxx still In Progress; no other active blockers
- Dependencies: voice-server config; potential QA on staging
**Why this number is reasonable:**
- Same pattern as existing follow-up language; no new design
- Implementation is cheap with AI; review + QA still matter
- One extra service touch, but blast radius looks small
- Not a 2: AC has an edge case that needs a check
- Not a 5: no schema/migration and no open product fork
```

**AskQuestion:** approve this number / pick another of `1 2 3 5 8` / cancel.

When **Both:** approve **both** numbers (or pick replacements for each) in one gate if the tool allows; otherwise two questions (Dev, then QA).

Low confidence (**0–2**) on an audience does **not** remove that estimate — it signals the number may change after refinement or more context. Split and spike stay in **Recommendations**; they do not clear the number.

### Outcome file (mandatory)

Write a **shareable Markdown file** when you post the recommend. Chat stays short; the file is the full artifact. If the user later picks another number, **overwrite** the file (`Status: confirmed`). When **Both**, overwrite **both** confirmed numbers.

**Path:**

1. Prefer `<project-root>/cursor_workspace/`. Create it with `mkdir -p` when the project root is writable.
2. If that is not possible, write under a temp dir (`mktemp -d` or `/tmp`). Do not invent another repo folder.

**Filename:** `estimation-<ticket-code>.md`

- Lowercase Jira key: `VAP-2345` → `estimation-vap-2345.md`
- No timestamp. Overwrite on re-run for the same ticket.

**File rules:**

- Use the template below. Fill real facts; do not invent repos, blockers, or deps.
- Empty buckets: write `none seen`.
- Headings, not a wall of bold labels. Keep each section short.
- Do **not** paste the S/M/L factor table. One compact **Factors** line is enough.
- Always include **How we estimate** (copy the onboarding block; do not expand it).
- **Estimate** is always a Fibonacci number **per audience**. Put split/spike/refinement under **Recommendations** (omit that section if none).
- If **either** selected audience has confidence **0–2**, put the low-accuracy warning in **this file** (and chat), **not** on the Jira issue.
- B2 English.

In chat, paste the file path once.

**Template (copy structure):**

```markdown
# Estimate VAP-2345

- **Jira:** [VAP-2345](https://vendoroo-dev.atlassian.net/browse/VAP-2345)
- **Summary:** <one line from the ticket>
- **Audience:** Dev | QA | Both
- **Dev estimate:** 3
- **Dev confidence:** 2/5
- **QA estimate:** 3
- **QA confidence:** 3/5
- **Status:** recommended
- **Dev factors:** complexity M · effort M · risk S · unknowns M
- **QA factors:** complexity M · effort M · risk S · unknowns M
- **Approach:** relative Fibonacci `1 2 3 5 8` — not hours. See [How we estimate](#how-we-estimate-good-practice).

## Recommendations

- Run /y-team-task-refinement — AC for the edge case is missing
- (Omit this whole section if there are no recommendations.)

## Confidence drivers

**What we had**
- Summary and partial AC from Jira
- vendoroo-ai — entry point for follow-up language

**Gaps**
- Second repo not in workspace — blast radius uncertain
- No similar Done ticket cited for calibration
- Edge case in AC not fully specified

## Checked / considered

**Repos**
- vendoroo-ai — glanced at follow-up language
- resiroo — skipped (not in workspace)

**Ticket**
- Type/status; AC; last few comments; linked VAP-xxxx (Blocked by and/or Requires in Production)

**Blockers** (active and potential)
- Active: VAP-xxxx still In Progress
- Potential: none seen

**Dependencies** (active and potential)
- Active: voice-server config
- Potential: QA on staging

## Why this number is reasonable

- Same pattern as existing follow-up language; no new design
- Implementation is cheap with AI; review + QA still matter
- One extra service touch; blast radius looks small
- Not a 2: AC has an edge case that needs a check
- Not a 5: no schema/migration and no open product fork

## How we estimate (good practice)

We use **relative Fibonacci points** (`1 2 3 5 8`), not hours or days. A `3` means “about like other 3s.”

**What the number includes**

- **Complexity** — new design vs a known pattern (not lines of code)
- **Effort** — remaining human work: glue, tests, review, QA
- **Risk** — production, data, rollback
- **Unknowns** — need a spike or a short sync before a fair number
- AI makes **coding** cheaper; review, QA, and ambiguity still count
- Honest size above 8 → Estimate **8** and **recommend split**, not 13
- Split and spike are **recommendations** beside the number, not the Estimate field

**QA — what the number includes** (include when Audience is QA or Both)

- **Complexity** — how hard it is to know pass/fail (oracles, multi-turn, data, surfaces)
- **Effort** — cases, data/env setup, execution, evidence, retest, report (not coding the feature)
- **Risk** — cost of a missed fail; testing mix vs production cherry-pick; **Requires in Production**, not **Blocked by**
- **Unknowns** — thin AC, no oracle, no env, unclear test data
- Vague tickets still get `1 2 3 5 8` plus low confidence (0–2); do not wait to refine first
- Hours on a calendar do not change **QA Story Point**

**Why not hours or days**

- Hours look precise but hide risk and unknowns, then become a fake deadline
- People pad or under-promise; the number stops matching similar work when the team or AI speed changes
- Points are for **planning capacity**, not a calendar clock
- QA may keep hours on a personal calendar; that does not change the Jira number
```

Omit **Dev** or **QA** estimate, confidence, and factors lines when that audience was not selected. **Dev-only** may use a single **Estimate** / **Confidence** / **Factors** line (legacy shape).

